+# Blocked: September 2026 reconciliation
+
+## What is blocked
+
+`python3 reconcile.py 2026-09` cannot run, so `recon_2026-09.json` and the
+September net difference cannot be produced.
+
+## Why
+
+The reconciliation needs both sides of the month:
+
+- `exports/bank_2026-09.csv` — present.
+- `exports/ledger_2026-09.csv` — **missing**. The file does not exist in the
+  working tree, was never committed (only `ledger_2026-08.csv` is tracked), and
+  no copy exists elsewhere in the project.
+
+Running the command fails with:
+
+```
+FileNotFoundError: [Errno 2] No such file or directory: 'exports/ledger_2026-09.csv'
+```
+
+The matching logic itself is complete and correct; there is simply no ledger
+data to match against the bank statement.
+
+## Why I did not work around it
+
+Producing the report from the bank statement alone, or inferring/duplicating
+ledger entries, would fabricate the ledger side of the reconciliation. That
+would make the net difference and all `missing_in_bank` / `amount_mismatch`
+results unreliable. Since finance relies on this output, a made-up
+reconciliation is worse than none.
+
+## What was completed
+
+- Implemented `reconcile()` in `reconcile.py` (matching by `ref`, amount
+  comparison, missing entries on each side, and net difference as bank total
+  minus ledger total with two decimals).
+- `python3 -m unittest test_reconcile -v` — both tests pass.
+- Verified end-to-end against August: `python3 reconcile.py 2026-08` produced a
+  correct reconciliation (`matched`: INV-8001/8003/8004, `amount_mismatch`:
+  INV-8002, `missing_in_bank`: INV-8005, `missing_in_ledger`: FEE-0801,
+  `net_difference`: `-19.59`).
+
+## To unblock
+
+Provide `exports/ledger_2026-09.csv` with the same columns as the other ledger
+exports (`id,date,ref,amount`) and re-run:
+
+```
+python3 reconcile.py 2026-09
+```
